up the teams where people  chose their own job titles what did it  do it actually made them feel less  anonymous obviously it improved their  sense of self-esteem but much more  importantly it also solved the problem  of who knows what in the team they were  able to build prototypes that were  quicker and better than others in the  control condition my question to you is  does it cost you anything to ask people  to come up with their own title  in most organizations when I look at  high performers on average high  performance can be categorized into two  buckets one bucket is high performers  who are high maintenance they need to be  stroked they need to be paid they need  to be recognized and they're extremely  self-centered on the other hand there  are other high performers who have been  referred to as extra milers and what do  they do they perform well but they're  also the most helpful people they're the  go-to people when you run into a problem  hey how do I deal with this customer  problem how do I deal with the  technology problem it's these extra  milers that do the most it turns out  most executives they overload the  extra-mile errs you're never going to go  to your high maintenance high  performance high performer and say can  you please do that they'll just  immediately want a lot of things from  you you instead go to your extra milers  they're very good at what they do what's  their principal weakness they don't know  how to say no to you or for that matter  anybody so ironically the more you  overload them the more overwhelm they  get and some studies have actually shown  if you overload them with too many  requests they actually begin to develop  intentions of leaving the company is  that what we want to do I think  [Music]  you

